058,0.065,0.068,0.079,0.095,0.076,0.043,0.034,0.026,0.012,0.022,0.024,0.093,0.019,0.058,0.019,0.014,0.03,0.065,0.056,0.064,0.094,0.033,0.072,0.124,0.089,0.009,0.02,0.014,0.023,0.018,0.025,0.051,0.051,0.028,0.05,0.029,0.115,0.169,0.117,0.076,0.06,0.049,0.084,0.071,0.053,0.099,0.066,0.027,0.055,0.066,0.15,0.089,0.027,0.024,0.011,0.061,0.08,0.059,0.033,0.05,0.06,0.071,0.097,0.077,0.144,0.051,0.043,0.082,0.067,0.066,0.026,0.019,0.032,0.006,0.01,0.065,0.026,0.019,0.03,0.034,0.083,0.052,0.03,0.111,0.068,0.032,0.061,0.082,0.068,0.035,0.047,0.076,0.042,0.053,0.081,0.006,0.016,0.033,0.072,0.063,0.04,0.029,0.064,0.073,0.037,0.033,0.045,0.01,0.019,0.043,0.13,0.088,0.057,0.054,0.032,0.059,0.076,0.101,0.049,0.051,0.059,0.031,0.044,0.048,0.018,0.02,0.094,0.113,0.004,0.026,0.044,0.026,0.064,0.07,0.083,0.057,0.046,0.048,0.039,0.037,0.064,0.098,0.04,0.039,0.12,0.052,0.025,0.016,0.098,0.049,0.03]}},"alerts":[{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued September 28 at 11:03AM MDT until September 30 at 6:00AM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of Colorado, including the following areas, Animas\nRiver Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, Northwest San Juan\nMountains, Paradox Valley/Lower Dolores River, San Juan River\nBasin, Southwest San Juan Mountains and Uncompahgre Plateau and\nDallas Divide and southeast Utah, including the following areas,\nCanyonlands/Natural Bridges, La Sal and Abajo Mountains and\nSoutheast Utah.\n\n* WHEN...Through late Tuesday night.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding will be possible over burn scars, at low-water\ncrossings, and along creeks, streams, washes, arroyos and other\nflood-prone locations. Creeks and streams may rise out of their\nbanks. Unpaved roads could become muddy and impassable.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Periods of heavy rainfall, associated with remnant moisture from\nHurricane Polo, are expected to produce numerous showers and\nembedded thunderstorms. Heavy rain falling on saturated soils and\nburn scars could lead to localized flash flooding and rises on\nlocal rivers.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-09-30T06:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Uncompahgre Plateau/Dallas Divide; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Paradox Valley/Lower Dolores River; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.ed9ede493b5b1546377a5ccc5f8c64d7cb67cd19.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.ed9ede493b5b1546377a5ccc5f8c64d7cb67cd19.001.1"},{"event":"Hydrologic Outlook","headline":"Hydrologic Outlook issued September 27 at 8:56PM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"ESFGJT\n\nStrong southwesterly winds ahead of a closed low centered over\nCalifornia will pull tropical moisture from Tropical Storm Odalys and\nHurricane Polo towards eastern Utah and western Colorado, with\natmospheric moisture peaking in our area on Monday. The northerly\nextent of the expected excessive rainfall poses a threat to our\nsouthern San Juan river basins. Current forecasts suggest 1 to 4\ninches of rain Monday morning through early Wednesday. At face value,\nrivers and tributaries will see a rise due to the forecast rainfall.\nHowever, localized heavier amounts could result in flooding across\nthe southern San Juan foothills and communities further downstream.\n\nRivers have responded rapidly to recent rainfall due to saturated\nsoils and increased runoff potential. Thus, prolonged periods of\nadditional rainfall over these regions would trigger a similar\nresponse. The current precipitation forecast suggests larger peaks in\nseveral creeks and rivers than what has been recently observed.\nHowever, high end probabilistic rainfall amounts (i.e. above 90th\npercentile) may lead to rivers exceeding action stage to minor\nflooding. Most probable basins to see significant rises include; the\nRio Blanco, San Juan, Piedra, Los Pinos/Vallecito Creek, and the\nAnimas River Basins.\n\nConvective storms and periods of heavy rainfall fed from said\ntropical moisture could result in localized flooding impacts across\nthe CWA. However, confidence decreases rapidly north of the San\nJuans. Confidence has increased enough for a Flood Watch to be issued\nfor southern portions of the forecast area valid from 6AM Monday\nthrough 6AM Wednesday. Continue to monitor the latest forecasts\nespecially for those who reside and/or plan to recreate in the Watch\nareas.","ends":"2026-09-28T18:00:00-06:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Dolores, CO; Gunnison, CO; Hinsdale, CO; La Plata, CO; Mesa, CO; Montezuma, CO; Montrose, CO; Ouray, CO; San Juan, CO; San Miguel, CO; Grand, UT; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.e1c00eb778d71aa459bdceb0073ca129d8221788.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.e1c00eb778d71aa459bdceb0073ca129d8221788.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-09-28T19:57:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 281957 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 157 PM MDT Mon Sep 28 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Scattered to widespread precipitation showers and storms are expected through midweek. - This rainfall increases flooding concerns, especially within and adjacent to the southern San Juans. - A Flood Watch is in effect for southeast Utah and southwest Colo","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-09-28T19:09:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION...Deep moisture will continue to stream in from the southwest bringing widespread rain and a few embedded thunderstorms over the next few days. A Flood Watch has been issued for southeast UT and southwest CO due to the increased rainfall and flooding risks. All 